he repeat and the  flanking regions  and then the fourth scenario was that we  saw no increase in alleles when  comparing to a traditional pcrce  approach or a length-based approach  showing you the data and how we  visualized it to fully understand  sequence variation we have a locus here  and you can see that everything is  color-coded  where each nucleotide has a different  color  but we could easily visualize any trends  or any snip variation that might be  present in the data  any black dots are deletions or missing  data  in that particular string sequence if we  zoom  in you can see in the orange box we have  the flanking region  and then at the top you have that repeat  region start  and you can see in that first repeat for  this particular locus  there's quite a bit of sequence  diversity  present and this sequence diversity has  been characterized  in the literature and so it wasn't  uncommon to observe it  but essentially tabulating the data in  this way  allowed for easy recognition of sequence  variations that we could best  understand each locus that we were  studying  what this study really revealed and why  we thought it was very significant to  share with the forensic community  is that there's a substantial gain in  information using a sequence based  approach  so you can see here the y chromosome the  x chromosome  and the autosomal strs all showed  an extensive gain in information using  sequence-based alleles  demonstrated in orange over length-based  alleles demonstrated in purple  focusing in on d9s one one two two  i really want to point out how  significantly impactful  sequence variation can be so we're going  to focus  in on the purple bar which is the allele  12.  zooming in you can see that there are  three different  alleles sequence based alleles  represented for the length-based allele  12.  the motif defined in the middle is  referring simply to that repeat region  and if you shift your attention to the  motif with flanking region sequences  you can see that highlighted in red and  highlighted in yellow  is the uniqueness of adding in that  flanking region information  so we went from one length based allele  12  to now three sequence based allele  12.  breaking this down for you the 12  length based allele will present as a  peak single peak  12 on an electro ferrogram however if  you look at the sequence you can see  that there's quite a bit of variability  looking only at the repeat region you  have two unique alleles  so the tag 12 or you have the  tag1tc ga1  tag10 and then you gain an additional  allele when you add in that flanking  region information  so this is an example of a locus that  when using both the repeat  and the flanking region information you  have a substantial gain in the total  number of alleles present  what's interesting is that looking at  the totality of the d9 locus we went  from  eight length-based alleles to 17  sequence-based alleles so that's a  drastic improvement in the overall power  of discrimination of those alleles at  that locus  visualizing what i just explained and  going back to that initial  image of sequence variation from the  beginning of my talk you can see here  where that variability falls within the  locus  so typically that snip is found at the  beginning of the repeat region  so it's highlighted in blue and you can  see it with the purple arrow  and that flanking region variation is  characterized by the orange arrow and  does have that db snip record associated  with it  meaning that it has been observed by  other groups  so what can happen when you only look at  the repeat region because  we spend all of this time really  focusing on characterizing alleles  based on their repeat region repeat  region composition  remembering that pcr ce  captures that entire amplicon so those  primers are set outside of the repeat  region capturing flanking region  information on either side of the repeat  even though we don't report it that way  we're still capturing that information  and changes in the flanking region will  impact the  actual length-based allele call if  there's an insertion a deletion  etc when we think about using mps and if  we only focus on the repeat region we're  potentially losing that  important information that's captured  and  quantified in the flanking region so  as we move forward using an mps based  approach it's very important to be  consistent with our pcrce methods  of looking at the entire amplicon  and defining both the repeat and the  flanking region  in terms of what the totality of allele  composition  is so just a couple of examples to  really reinforce this point  looking at d7 if you look at the repeat  region  allele 11 by length  only the mps software so in this case we  use the forensic universal analysis  software  reports only tatc 11.  however when doing concordant studies  with pcrce we found that it was at 10.3  and only in looking at the flanking  region information  were we able to capture a deletion in  the five prime  flank in order to explain this  microvariant or the 10.3 allele  designation  so again just reinforcing the value of  looking at the totality of information  captured  another example looking at d22 the 15  allele  the forensic software denoted just the  repeat region calling it a 15  whereas concordance data with capillary  electrophoresis and then using our  in-house tools  demonstrated that it was actually a 15.1  because of an insertion in the five  prime flank  of the amplicon so again just  stressing the value and importance of  looking at the totality of the amplicon  information  as i come to a close i want to just  bring your attention to the population  genetic consequences of using an  mps-based approach  really we're gaining so much information  in terms of the diversity of alleles  using an nps based approach but it will  also  strengthen our statistical support for  our profiles in terms of the rarity  of particular alleles and then the  rarity of the overall profile  and then looking at population specific  differences and inferring potential  ancestral information  from the unique sequences or the  the rare alleles that we're capturing in  actual casework so here i presented just  looking at the  overall random match probability of the  autosomal loci  from a length versus a sequence based  approach and you can see that there are  multifold differences  within each of our four populations  using either approach  taking that a step further there are  obvious markers that  either drastically increase the  diversity  of a dna profile and have a significant  impact on the rarity  of that profile and therefore impact the  random match probability and there are  other  loci where we learned didn't have any  real advantage using a sequence based  approach  and so they didn't significantly change  the power of discrimination  for their alleles in a dna profile and  those are highlighted here  so for a sequence sequence-based  improvement  you have certain alleles highlighted in  green that  are really going to take your dna  profile to the next level in terms of  power of discrimination  and then highlighted in red  where there is no significant  gain in information using those loci  because their length-based designations  seem to be  sufficiently uh powerful or  sufficiently useful for inferring  the rarity of those alleles  some final thoughts just pulling  all of this information together we know  that  using a length-based approach pcrce is a  robust way to generate a forensic dna  profile  however as we continue to push the  boundaries of  our sequence-based research it's very  important to keep in mind that a  sequence based approach does offer  significant promise  and potential for advancement in our  field of forensic genetics  looking at the overall improvement of  diversity or variation at particular  loci  in the study that i talked about in this  presentation we saw an increase in 644  alleles using a sequence based approach  and that is extremely significant  that just speaks to how much information  that we're not capturing using pcr  ce of those 400 were novel  meaning that they were never yet or they  were never before characterized in the  literature so that was a really  wonderful learning opportunity for our  group to really understand  the amount of diversity that hasn't yet  been  captured in the forensic genetic  research  and then finally it allowed us to  identify that certain loci  aren't substantially more powerful  using a sequence based approach and that  potentially we have the opportunity to  explore  new loci that might better aid us  in our complex interpretations moving  forward  but as the literature has supported  there have been continued efforts to  characterize  our forensic loci using a sequence based  approach  and there is a compendium of data  including the star seek  database housed by the national  institute of standards and technology  available to really understand the  sequence diversity in our loci  a great um publication that highlights  what i discussed  in today's talk um came out in 2016.  so please check out that publication if  you're interested in learning more about  exactly what our study entailed and  just to provide you with a variety of  resources in better understanding  sequence diversity  and i'd just like to thank my team  at the university of north texas health  science center  where i completed my doctoral studies  and did  the totality of the data presented in  this talk  as well as illumina and the national  institute of justice who supported this  research  if you have any questions please feel  free to reach out to me i am  again at the university of toronto  mississauga  um working in the forensic science  program and i'm very happy to talk more  about  str sequence variation forensic genetics  or forensic biology in general  thank you


